# Attention Drift Detector — Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
A webcam-based attention monitoring tool for deep work and study sessions.
Detects attention drift (zoning out, visual distraction, mind-wandering) in real time
using gaze estimation and head pose tracking — no eye-tracking hardware required.
Designed as a smarter, data-driven alternative to Pomodoro timers.

## Stack
- Python 3.11+
- MediaPipe Face Mesh for facial landmark tracking
- OpenCV for webcam capture and frame processing
- dlib or L2CS-Net for gaze direction estimation
- PyTorch for attention state classification
- SQLite for session storage
- Plotly or Tkinter for real-time dashboard
- FastAPI (optional) for local dashboard server

## Architecture
```
capture/
  └── WebcamCapture           # OpenCV camera loop, frame normalization
  └── PrivacyFilter           # ensure no raw frames are persisted to disk
tracking/
  └── FaceMeshExtractor       # MediaPipe 468-landmark face mesh
  └── HeadPoseEstimator       # pitch/yaw/roll from facial landmarks (PnP solver)
  └── GazeEstimator           # gaze direction from iris landmarks or L2CS-Net
  └── BlinkDetector           # blink rate and duration (EAR metric)
state_estimation/
  └── AttentionClassifier     # multi-feature → attention state (focused/drifting/absent)
  └── TemporalSmoother        # EMA smoothing on raw frame predictions
  └── DriftEventDetector      # sustained drift detection with configurable threshold
session/
  └── SessionTracker          # session start/stop, attention timeline recording
  └── SessionDB               # SQLite: sessions, attention_events, frame_features
  └── SessionReport           # post-session stats: focus time, drift frequency, heatmap
alerts/
  └── NudgeSystem             # gentle audio/visual nudge on drift detection
  └── NudgeThrottler          # minimum time between nudges, configurable
ui/
  └── dashboard.py            # real-time attention bar + session timeline
```

## Coding Conventions
- PRIVACY FIRST: zero raw frames persisted — only extracted features and labels
- Head pose angles stored as (pitch, yaw, roll) float32 tuples in degrees
- Gaze direction as unit vector (gaze_x, gaze_y, gaze_z) in camera space
- Attention states: "focused", "drifting", "absent" — no ambiguous labels
- EAR (Eye Aspect Ratio) for blink: threshold at 0.25 for closed eye detection
- All frame processing functions are stateless — session state tracked separately
- Use dataclasses: FrameFeatures, AttentionEvent, SessionRecord, DriftAlert
- Type hints everywhere
- Inference pipeline must run under 33ms per frame to maintain 30fps

## ML-Specific Rules
- Input features per frame: head yaw, head pitch, gaze_x, gaze_y, blink flag, face visibility confidence
- Do not use gaze alone — head pose + gaze together are more reliable
- Face visibility confidence: if MediaPipe confidence < 0.7, mark frame as "absent" (face not visible)
- Attention classifier: lightweight MLP or SVM — not a transformer (latency constraint)
- Temporal model: use a sliding window of 30 frames (1 second at 30fps) as input, not single frames
- Drift event = sustained drifting state for > N consecutive seconds (default 5s, configurable)
- Calibration: first 30 seconds of session captures "focused" baseline gaze and head pose ranges per user

## Head Pose Thresholds (Configurable)
- Yaw > ±25 degrees: looking away from screen
- Pitch > +20 degrees: looking up (ceiling); < -30 degrees: looking down (phone)
- Roll is informational only — not used for attention classification
- All thresholds stored in config, not hardcoded

## Privacy Rules (Non-Negotiable)
- No video recording of any kind
- No frames written to disk — features only
- Webcam stream processed in memory, discarded per frame
- User can pause tracking at any time — hard pause, not soft
- Session data stored locally only, deletable on demand
- README must document exactly what is captured

## What to Avoid
- Do not use face recognition or identity detection — this is not a surveillance tool
- Do not alert on every single drift frame — throttle nudges aggressively
- Do not classify attention from a single frame — always use temporal window
- No cloud dependency — all inference runs locally
- Do not mistake head-down reading posture for distraction — calibrate pitch baseline per user
- Do not log session data without explicit user consent flow on first launch

## Nudge Design Rules
- Nudges must be non-intrusive: soft chime or subtle screen border flash
- No pop-ups or interruptions that break flow more than drift already did
- Nudge cooldown: minimum 3 minutes between nudges (configurable)
- After 3 nudges without recovery, offer a break suggestion instead

## File Naming
- snake_case everywhere
- Sessions: sessions/{user_id}/{session_id}.db
- Session report: reports/{session_id}_{timestamp}.json
- Model weights: models/attention_classifier_v{version}.pt

## Key Metrics to Track
- Frame processing latency (target under 33ms)
- Attention state classification accuracy vs. manual labels
- Drift detection recall (what fraction of actual drift episodes are caught)
- False positive rate (nudges during confirmed focused periods)
- Focus time percentage per session
